---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadReport } from '../lib/report';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
import { REPO_URL, REPO_BENCH_PATH } from '../../site.config.mjs';
import { int, isoDate } from '../lib/format';

const { bundle, gate, source } = loadReport();
const p = bundle.provenance;

const repoBench = `${REPO_URL}/tree/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}`;
const schemaUrl = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/schemas/report.schema.json`;
const recordSchemaUrl = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/schemas/run-record.schema.json`;
const wilsonUrl = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/harness/v2sbench/stats/wilson.py`;
const armsUrl = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/harness/v2sbench/arms.py`;
const siteUrl = `${REPO_URL}/tree/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/site`;

const auditChecks = [
  {
    title: 'Is the pre-registration frozen?',
    body: `The provenance block records the pre-registration commit. If it is empty, or the status line says the pre-registration is not frozen, the pre-registration's own instruction applies: treat the numbers as exploratory. This build says "${bundle.status.prereg_frozen ? 'frozen' : 'not yet frozen'}".`,
  },
  {
    title: 'Is this real data?',
    body: `Check provenance.is_mock in the bundle and the status banner on every page. This build reports is_mock = ${p.is_mock === true}, status.kind = "${bundle.status.kind}", rendered as "${gate.kind}". The report builder refuses to aggregate mock and real records, so a figure can never be a blend of the two.`,
  },
  {
    title: 'Do the arithmetic yourself',
    body: 'verify-results recomputes every published figure from the immutable run records and prints every disagreement; silence means the arithmetic checks out. The statistics are kept to the standard library for exactly this reason: an audit that first requires installing a scientific stack is one nobody performs. Separately, this site recomputes each Wilson interval from the published successes and n at build time and marks any row where its own result disagrees with the bundle, so a mismatch shows up on the page rather than in a log.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Did the suite change under the figure?',
    body: 'Every trial record carries the task-suite digest, the harness digest and the container image digest. A figure whose suite digest differs from the suite in your checkout is not a figure about your checkout.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Were the baselines real?',
    body: 'The baseline prompt is committed at tasks/_prompts/baseline.md and is byte-identical across arms. Read it. A weak baseline is the standard way to manufacture a benchmark win, and the only defence a reader has is being able to see the prompt.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Could the skills have seen the checkers?',
    body: 'Run v2sbench validate --blind. It rejects any arm material containing a declared forbidden literal, a checker or oracle filename, or a mined string literal from either. CI runs it on every change.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Do the tasks work at all?',
    body: 'Run the oracle gate and the trivial-agent gate. Every task ships an oracle that must pass the checker in a fresh environment, and a null agent that must score 0. Independent measurements put the broken-task rate in borrowed agent benchmarks at 10 to 15%.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Reproduce"
  description="How to run v2s-bench yourself, and how to audit every figure on this site back to the immutable run records."
>
  <div class="stack">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Auditability</p>
      <h1>Reproduce it, or audit it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This benchmark is run by the authors of the tool it grades. That is a reason to
        check the numbers rather than read them, so the whole chain is designed to be
        re-run by a stranger: the suite is in the repository, the trial records are
        immutable and published, and the harness&rsquo;s <code>verify-results</code>
        command recomputes every figure on this site from those records.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="chain">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="chain">What produced this page</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">Four steps, each inspectable</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="chain">
        <li>
          <strong>Trials.</strong> One immutable JSON record per (task, arm, seed),
          validating against <a href={recordSchemaUrl}>run-record.schema.json</a>. Records
          are append-only: a correction is a new record, never an edit. Three conventions
          matter when reading them: <code>status: error</code> is infrastructure breakage
          and is excluded from the endpoint with its rate published per arm, never counted
          as a task failure; <code>status: refused</code> means the material failed the
          leakage gate and was deliberately not scored; and <code>is_mock: true</code>
          marks stub-agent and gate records, which the report builder refuses to aggregate
          with real ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Aggregation.</strong> <code>report</code> reduces the records
          to one bundle validating against <a href={schemaUrl}>report.schema.json</a>.
          Verdicts are computed here, from the pre-registered failure conditions in
          <a href={armsUrl}>arms.py</a>, never written by hand.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rendering.</strong> This site reads that bundle and nothing else. It has
          no database, no API and no second source of numbers. The bundle it read for this
          build is <code>{source.split('/').slice(-3).join('/')}</code>, generated{' '}
          {isoDate(bundle.generated_at)}, carrying {int(p.trials_total ?? 0)} trials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audit.</strong> <code>verify-results</code> recomputes the bundle from
          the records and prints every disagreement. The bundle this page was
          built from is served at <a href={href('/report.json')}>report.json</a>, so you
          can diff it against your own run.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="run">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="run">Run the benchmark</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">Docker and Python 3.11+</p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Tasks run offline in throwaway containers; no task requires a destructive,
        networked or credentialed action. The harness is deliberately
        dependency-light so a reader can audit the arithmetic without installing a
        scientific stack.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`git clone ${REPO_URL}
cd video-to-skill/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}

cd harness
python3 -m pip install -e .        # pyyaml and jsonschema; no scientific stack

# integrity gates first: a suite that fails these measures nothing
python3 -m v2sbench.cli validate --blind   # no material carries checker or oracle strings
python3 -m v2sbench.cli gates              # oracle must pass, trivial agent must score 0

# hermetic videos are rendered at run time, never committed
python3 -m v2sbench.cli render

# headroom before outcomes: probe each task's knowledge gap cold
python3 -m v2sbench.cli probe

# the headline comparison, then the ablations
python3 -m v2sbench.cli run --armset core
python3 -m v2sbench.cli run --armset ablation

# aggregate the immutable records into one bundle, then audit it
python3 -m v2sbench.cli report --out ../results/report.json
python3 -m v2sbench.cli verify-results`}</code></pre>
      <p class="note">
        Those are the entry points the repository documents; run
        <code>python3 -m v2sbench.cli --help</code> in your checkout for what that
        version implements. The harness is v0.1 and still being built, and every figure
        on this site carries the <code>harness_version</code> that produced it, so a
        figure can never be silently attributed to a different one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Arm sets are named in <a href={armsUrl}>arms.py</a> and can also be given as a
        comma list. An arm is only ever a choice of context material: the model, the
        tools, the sandbox, the budget and the task prompt are held identical by
        construction, because the moment an arm differs in anything else the comparison
        stops meaning anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="site">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="site">Build this site against your own results</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">One bundle in, static files out</p>
      </div>
      <pre><code>{`cd ${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/site
pnpm install

# reads ../results/report.json; falls back to the committed
# no-results bundle when that file does not exist
pnpm build

# or point it anywhere
V2S_BENCH_REPORT=/path/to/your/report.json pnpm build

# base path for the two deployment homes, one value each
V2S_BENCH_BASE=/bench   V2S_BENCH_ORIGIN=https://v2s.dev       pnpm build
V2S_BENCH_BASE=/        V2S_BENCH_ORIGIN=https://bench.v2s.dev pnpm build`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The build fails rather than publishing something misleading. If the bundle&rsquo;s
        <code>schema_version</code> is not the one this site renders, it refuses to guess
        at the difference; and if a bundle that reports no results still carries
        measurements after sanitisation, a build-time assertion stops the build instead of
        letting a figure through. The <a href={siteUrl}>site source</a> is about a dozen
        components and no chart library: the figures are hand-rolled inline SVG, so the
        page ships no client-side JavaScript except a small progressive-enhancement script
        that makes the task table sortable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="honesty">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="honesty">What this site will not do</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">Enforced in the loader, not in the templates</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="spaced">
        <li>
          <strong>It will not draw a figure it has no data for.</strong> When the bundle
          reports no results, the loader strips the arms, the cells, the strata, the effect
          sizes and the probe scores before any page sees them, and forces every verdict to
          <em>insufficient data</em>. The pages then render empty states rather than zeros.
          A build-time assertion fails the build if anything survives that strip.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It will not quietly render mock data.</strong> A mock bundle is banner-ed
          on every page, watermarked inside every figure, and labelled in the provenance
          block. If <code>provenance.is_mock</code> and <code>status.kind</code> disagree,
          the more cautious reading wins and the disagreement is printed on the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It will not show a rate without an interval.</strong> Where the bundle
          omits the Wilson bounds they are recomputed from successes and n; where it
          publishes bounds that disagree with that recomputation, the row is marked.
          Secondary rates published as bare fractions get an interval derived from the
          row&rsquo;s n, marked as derived, or no interval and a note saying why.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It will not style a null result as a failure.</strong>
          <em>not supported</em> renders at exactly the weight of <em>supported</em>: same
          surface, same border, same type size, no colour. A null result is a result, and
          the pre-registration commits to publishing it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="audit">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="audit">A sceptic&rsquo;s checklist</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">In the order worth doing it</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          auditChecks.map((check) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{check.title}</dt>
              <dd>{check.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contracts">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 id="contracts">The contracts</h2>
        <p class="eyebrow">Everything this page depends on</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="links">
        <li><a href={schemaUrl}>report.schema.json</a> &mdash; what this site renders, field for field.</li>
        <li><a href={recordSchemaUrl}>run-record.schema.json</a> &mdash; one immutable record per trial.</li>
        <li><a href={armsUrl}>arms.py</a> &mdash; the arms and the pre-registered comparisons.</li>
        <li><a href={wilsonUrl}>stats/wilson.py</a> &mdash; the interval convention, shared with the parent project.</li>
        <li><a href={repoBench}>v2s-bench/</a> &mdash; the suite, the harness, the pre-registration and this site.</li>
        <li><a href={href('/report.json')}>report.json</a> &mdash; the bundle this build rendered.</li>
        <li>
          <code>results/</code> &mdash; the raw records, the probe answers and the bundle.
          They are git-ignored by default so a local run does not dirty the tree; a
          published run is committed deliberately by the workflow that produced it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        Independent replication is invited. If you run this suite and get a different
        answer, that is the most useful thing that could happen to it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .chain {
    counter-reset: step;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .chain li {
    position: relative;
    padding: 0.55rem 0 0.55rem 2.3rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .chain li::before {
    counter-increment: step;
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.62rem;
    width: 1.5rem;
    height: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .spaced li + li {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .links {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .links li {
    padding: 0.33rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
